g in self-defense, but with strict limits—protect innocents, honor treaties, pursue peace when possible. Extremist ideology works by ripping those verses out of context and ignoring centuries of scholarly interpretation that emphasize peace and justice. 4. **The echo chamber effect.** If everyone you know shares the same impression, that’s often a sign that you’re all drawing from the same limited sources, not that the impression is accurate. It can be tough to break out of that, but a religious studies course is exactly the kind of place where you’ll encounter primary sources—what Muslims actually read, what they say about their own faith, and the historical contexts that shaped it. You’ll get to see the gap between the news headline version and the lived reality of a vast, diverse tradition. So it’s not that the terrorism isn’t real—it is, and it’s horrific. It’s that those acts don’t define the religion any more than the Inquisition defines Christianity, or the Lord’s Resistance Army defines indigenous African religions. Taking the course won’t force you to agree with Islam’s teachings, but it will give you the tools to separate political extremism from a faith that’s guided billions of people toward charity, hospitality, and a search for God. That’s a far more complete—and accurate—picture.
