he entirety of the evidence—the conquests, the verses, the oppressive regimes—and decide that, on balance, this is a religion with a violence problem that outweighs its other elements. That’s a defensible judgment. But what a course offers is the ability to make that judgment *from knowledge rather than from a curated set of worst cases*. It’s the difference between judging a novel by the most violent paragraph, torn out and read alone, and actually reading the whole thing—and then still deciding it’s a violent book. At least then your conclusion is based on solid ground, not on a steady diet of the ugliest excerpts. I’m not asking you to like Islam. I’m asking you to consider that the mountain of data might be more complicated than “the wolf shows itself,” and that the same tools you’d use to defend a complex view of your own tradition—or any tradition you know intimately—might apply here as well. The professor wouldn’t be explaining away the mountain; they’d be showing you its geology, so you can see what actually built it.
